#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Read-only RELEASE_CANDIDATE_V1 admission. (CC V1-08 / #103)

What it answers
---------------
"May this version be treated as deployable at all?" -- from artifacts that already
exist, without creating anything.

    admission = ACCEPT    every identity below is established and nothing conflicts
    admission = REJECT    a rule refuses this candidate
    admission = UNKNOWN   an input the decision needs was not supplied

Guessing is the failure mode this replaces
------------------------------------------
The Command Center used to have no definition of what a compliant candidate is, so
a Boss PR that only half-described itself could reach the deploy chain and be
discovered there. Admission is that definition, placed *before* TEST_PR / VERIFY /
deploy readiness: a version that cannot establish its own source, build, artifact,
test and rollback identities is refused here, by name, and the upstream fixes it.
This component never completes a missing field, never substitutes a branch for a
commit, and never widens a rule to fit a historical PR.

What it reads (read-only, and never anything else)
--------------------------------------------------
    --candidate          the canonical candidate document, whose release_candidate_v1
                         block is the RELEASE_CANDIDATE_V1 being admitted. The
                         document's own source fields stay authoritative for the
                         source identity, and the block must agree with them: a
                         candidate cannot carry two source identities.
    --test-pr-evidence   OPTIONAL signed TEST_PR Evidence. It is the only proof that
                         the artifact came from that source, that the builder the
                         candidate names is the one that produced it, and that the
                         artifact still exists as a sealed package, so without it the
                         artifact binding cannot be established and the verdict is
                         UNKNOWN rather than ACCEPT.
    --plan               OPTIONAL approved deploy plan bundle. When supplied, the
                         candidate is cross-checked against what the plan approves.
    --go-repo            OPTIONAL GO checkout, used to confirm that the build
                         definition is the one actually staged for the executor
                         (the Dockerfile digest), rather than a claim.

What it can never do
--------------------
It holds no private key, signs nothing, creates or publishes no Task, opens no
request switch, accepts no caller-supplied image/service/path/environment/command,
contacts no host, and does not touch Production. Every one of those is a `false` in
the `authority_boundary` block of its own output.
"""
import argparse
import datetime as dt
import hashlib
import json
import pathlib
import re
import sys

